{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":450465,"num_turns":19,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"09540c49-be5d-4d0f-8132-cee240c34831","total_cost_usd":3.3511474999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":36,"cache_creation_input_tokens":152688,"cache_read_input_tokens":2018825,"output_tokens":32587,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":152688,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":777,"cache_read_input_tokens":166688,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1273},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":36,"outputTokens":32587,"cacheReadInputTokens":2018825,"cacheCreationInputTokens":152688,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.3511474999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**`diagnostics-workflow` — correction round done, `turn: codex`, commit `4a92e20`.**\n\nWhere we are: Codex assessed Unit 2 and froze three findings. This run corrected exactly those three and changed no file except the state file.\n\nAll three reproduced. What they turned into:\n\n- **Finding 2 — fully resolved.** Both SOP drafts exist and are readable at the `~/.codex/attachments/` paths Codex gave. The previous run's \"NOT AVAILABLE\" was wrong — it never looked there. Both read in full and sorted into three groups: what current rules already cover, what conflicts with current rules and is declined with a reason, and what is a real gap worth adopting.\n- **Findings 1 and 3 — resolved together, and they overturn the previous verdict's size.** I compared the two live commands against the seven behaviours the brief requires. Four of the seven are missing or too weak. Two of those four are backed by the repository's own written record, not by opinion: neither command requires proof that the fault is real before diagnosing it (four logged cases, one of which already names `/resolve-repo-problem` as the fix site), and `/resolve-incident` accepts \"quote the changed line\" as verification — which is the exact failure class the repo's logs call its most-logged problem, at 6–8+ recorded instances.\n\nSo the answer to Codex's third finding is **no**: fixing the two contract defects does not deliver the objective. The router hands over ownership only — `SKILL.md:312` says so directly — so a standard method has to live inside the two commands.\n\nThe verdict rung is unchanged (**improve an existing resource** — no new command, no merge of the two, no router change). Its scope is now five behavioural changes across **four** files, not two repairs across two. The extra two files are the incident template and the incident log, because the outcome vocabulary is written out in five places, which the previous boundary missed.\n\nTwo corrections to the earlier record worth flagging: the complexity budget was declared \"not engaged\" and is actually engaged (the change adds mandatory steps) — it clears on the evidenced-failure prong. And three further gaps had no evidence of real harm, so they are recorded as not-built-now with triggers that would reopen them, rather than built on a hunch.\n\nAssessment is Codex's move.","ttft_ms":2043,"ttft_stream_ms":1197,"time_to_request_ms":40,"type":"result","duration_ms":454288,"uuid":"60dcc4ed-e8d1-42ca-9d15-9f4e2f4cf46e"}
